I need your decision: two more probes would find out why 40-page documents lose extra accuracy, and they need 9 runs past the campaign's 87-run cap. All the evaluation work is finished and nothing is running.

## What the campaign found
These results are provisional. Scores come from the Qwen3-8B rubric judge (an LLM that grades each answer), paired per question, at seeds 42 and 43. The test set is LongDocURL: 1,407 questions, each on one real PDF. A "slot" is one compressed vector standing for 8 tokens of input.

| rank | cause | judge accuracy, compressed minus uncompressed (pt) | questions affected |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Compression itself, at every length | −17 to −20 already at the shortest size (10 pages, 10 mixed items, ~4k text tokens); the two seeds agree within 2 pt in every cell | ~233 of 1,407 on 10-page documents |
| 2 | Length, on page images only | 40 pages vs 10 pages loses a further −6.5 / −5.3 (rubric judge), −5.7 / −6.1 (a second, Yes/No judge prompt), −4.6 / −3.6 (string match) | ~80 of 1,407 at 40 pages |
| 3 | Training the compressor on 1,024-token chunks instead of 256 | hurts image pages; on long text the seeds disagree (+4.6 / 0.0), and the +4.6 comes from one weak 256-chunk model | ~65–93 of 1,500 on 4-page documents |

- **No length effect shown elsewhere:** 20 pages, mixed documents (each page given as image or as text) and plain text meet neither the loss bar nor the no-loss bar. Text has too few questions per length bucket to decide.
- **One scorer disagrees on rank 2.** Exact match (the whole answer string must be identical) flips the sign: +6.0 / +7.2. The uncompressed model writes longer sentences at 40 pages, so its exact-match score falls from .19 to .08 while its judge score barely moves (.67 to .66). By the campaign rule, any scorer flipping the sign marks the number "judge-sensitive". I think the fault is in exact match, but that call is yours.
- **The evaluation itself is sound:** nothing is truncated, both modes see the same pages, and the closed-book and hidden-fact checks pass. The closed-book re-run on current code gives exactly the old score (.164).
- **One problem in the shared evaluation script:** its 32-token answer limit cuts the uncompressed model's answers mid-sentence. On 300 trial questions, raising the limit to 256 tokens moved the uncompressed page score from .34 to .72. I filed this as a hand-off to the pisco chat, because changing it moves every campaign's baseline.

## What this wake did
- Checked all nine finished jobs: each completed in one attempt, every answer was judged, and slot counts match the predictions. One file has 5.08% of answers at the length limit against a 5% bar. Removing those questions leaves the result unchanged (+4.9 vs +4.6).
- Ran the second-judge check (6 minutes) on the six files behind the 40-page result.
- Rewrote `docs/longdoc_diagnosis.md`, drew the length figure at `expQ/longdoc/figures/length_curve.svg` (an SVG, because no plotting library is installed), and rewrote `STATE.md`.
- Wrote the question to you in `campaign_longdoc/BLOCKED.md`.

Budget: 85 of 87 runs used, about 90 of 114 GPU-hours.

## Your decision
| option | runs | GPU-hours | what you get |
|---|---|---|---|
| A. Fund both probes | +9 (cap 87 → 96) | ~13, staying inside the 114 already approved | Whether the 40-page loss comes from the decoder reading too many slots, or from slot positions it never saw in training. A position cause might be fixable without any training. |
| B. Close now | 0 | 0 | The ranked list above, with the cause of rank 2 left open |

I recommend **A**. Both probes are already in the campaign plan, they can be undone, and only the run count goes over the cap.
